<p>In the latest turn in the widening Middle East conflict, President Trump said the United States would strike and “obliterate” Iran’s power plants if Tehran does not fully reopen the Strait of Hormuz within 48 hours. The warning came as the strait remained largely closed to shipping because of threats and fears of further attacks, raising the risk of a broader regional confrontation and fresh disruption to global energy supplies. Iran responded by warning that if the United States attacks its fuel and energy infrastructure, it would target US energy, information technology and desalination infrastructure across the region. At the same time, fighting between Iran and Israel stretched into a fourth week, with Iranian strikes wounding about 100 people in Israel and reports suggesting the latest exchanges are pushing the conflict in a more dangerous direction, including attacks near sensitive energy and nuclear-linked sites. The sharp rhetoric came just a day after President Trump spoke about “winding down” the war, underscoring how quickly the crisis has swung back toward escalation even as the world remains focused on keeping a vital shipping lane open and limiting further harm to civilians and infrastructure.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/mGsHdPOZlG4YV2Fn5MLZVk7i3iGp6yWOXq-aQAdY7Med99Ri4wL9yzdKNR8bKsCB3bNoMST_R9g9C6TceEJU-IuG-2x8PegNeUFmBOfu88ah' alt='Cargo ships in the Gulf, near the Strait of Hormuz, as seen from northern Ras al-Khaimah amid the US-Israeli war on Iran, in United Arab Emirates, March 11, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Energy artery: The Strait of Hormuz carries about one-fifth of global oil and liquefied natural gas supplies, so any sustained disruption would directly threaten energy-importing economies far beyond the Gulf.</li><li>Price shock: The near-closure of Hormuz pushed European gas prices up as much as 35% last week, showing how quickly conflict around the waterway can raise household and industrial energy costs.</li><li>Regional spillover: Iran’s threats extended beyond US targets to regional desalination and IT infrastructure, signaling risks not just to fuel markets but also to water and digital systems in Gulf states.</li></ul><blockquote>If Iran doesn’t FULLY OPEN, WITHOUT THREAT, ‌the Strait of Hormuz, within 48 HOURS from this exact point in time, the United States of America will hit and obliterate their various POWER PLANTS, STARTING WITH THE BIGGEST ONE FIRST! - President Donald Trump</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>United States: President Trump cast reopening the Strait of Hormuz as an immediate demand and tied any failure to comply to direct US strikes on Iran’s power system. (<a href='https://www.ft.com/content/a0e724d8-8f21-4ef4-811a-5a1be06d647e'>Financial Times</a>)</li><li>Iran: Iran’s Khatam al-Anbiya military command said any US attack on Iranian fuel and energy infrastructure would trigger attacks on US energy, IT and desalination infrastructure in the region. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1984285/trump-threatens-iran-with-power-plant-strikes-over-hormuz-oil-blockade'>Dawn</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/united-states-canada/article/3347454/trump-issues-hormuz-ultimatum-threatens-obliterate-irans-power-plants'>Trump issues Hormuz ultimatum, threatens to ‘obliterate’ Iran’s power plants</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.rferl.org/a/iran-israel-war-nuclear-trump-natanz-dimona/33712419.html'>Trump Gives Tehran 48 Hours To Open Hormuz Or US Will &#x27;Obliterate&#x27; Iran&#x27;s Power Plants</a> - rferl.org</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/iran-war-tehran-vows-response-to-trump-s-hormuz-threats/live-76470940'>Iran war: Tehran vows response to Trump&#x27;s Hormuz threats</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2026/mar/22/middle-east-crisis-live-iran-war-trump-ultimatum-major-attack-strait-of-hormuz-open-israel-hit-tehran-retaliation'>Middle East crisis l